Subject: [PATCH 3/3] resolver: attribute RETURNED to the concrete object, not the return slot

APPLIES ON TOP OF 0002-escape-reasons.patch. Two files:

  resolver/ReworkedResolver  new static stampReturned(resolved), ~20 lines of code.
  main/Main                  calls it after resolution, before printReasons.

WHY
---
RETURNED is recorded in exactly one place today, ReworkedResolver.collectReasons, and only
on a <returnValue,0> StandardObject. That node exists only if the return's conditional
value survived into the summary, and usually it does not:

    setAsReturn -> cascadeCV -> EscapeStatus.addEscapeState(cv) -> purge()

    public void purge() {
        if (containsCV()) {
            if (doesEscape()) purgeAndEscape();      // <- the CV is deleted here
            else if (containsNoEscape()) status.remove(NoEscape.getInstance());
        }
    }

The CV survives if and only if the object had NOT already escaped, so the reason is
destroyed exactly when the multiplicity would have exceeded one. Measured before this
patch:

  * corpus/E09, which is returned AND stored to a static AND used as a monitor, reported
    two reasons rather than three. Its summary is {<internal,0>=[Escape]} with no CV at
    all, and the program has zero <returnValue,0> nodes.
  * across avrora, xalan, luindex, fop, h2 and pmd, 87,083 objects carry RETURNED and
    there is exactly ONE distinct reason set containing it: {RETURNED}.

The <returnValue,0> node is also not a heap object. It is one return slot per method, and
under the reported population filter (internal + external) it is not counted at all, which
is why RETURNED appeared in no figure.

WHERE THE INFORMATION SURVIVES
------------------------------
JReturnStmtHandler.returnedObjects is populated at the return statement itself, from
ptg.vars, and is a plain map rather than an EscapeState. purge() cannot reach it. So the
set of returned objects survives even for objects whose CV -- and whose dependency edge --
was destroyed. ReworkedResolver already reads that map at :340; this adds a second reader.

WHY AFTER RESOLUTION AND NOT AT THE RETURN SITE
-----------------------------------------------
The obvious placement is PointsToGraph.setAsReturn, which knows the concrete objects and
runs before purge() can destroy anything. THAT WAS TRIED AND IS WRONG.

"Flows to the return slot" does NOT imply Escape. Under MarkParamReturnEscaping == false a
parameter that is merely returned does not escape -- isEscapingParam() explicitly skips
return successors:

    for (StandardObject nxt: this.graph.get(sobj)) {
        if (isReturnObject(nxt)) continue;          // <- returning a param is not an escape
        if (isEscapingObject(nxt)) return true;
    }

Recording at setAsReturn therefore put RETURNED on 560 NoEscape objects on avrora (465
parameter, 95 external), breaking the invariant that a non-empty reason set implies Escape
-- the invariant both integrity checks depend on. The earlier draft of this patch claimed
soundness from "resolve() tests isReturnObject() first"; that test is on the <returnValue,0>
node, not on the objects flowing into it, and the two are not the same claim.

Running after resolution makes the doesEscape() guard exact.

WHAT IT DOES NOT CHANGE
-----------------------
No EscapeStatus is constructed, mutated, or read beyond doesEscape(). No summary entry is
written. The write goes only to the append-only EscapeReasons side map, which nothing in
the analysis reads. The verdict is unchanged by construction, and the .res line-multiset
check (constraint 1) is the test of that claim rather than an argument for it.

It does not add an argument-side twin. "Passed as argument k to M" does NOT imply escape --
it is exactly the conditional the dependency edge already encodes -- so recording it as an
EscapeReason would put reasons on NoEscape objects, which is the same bug described above.
The argument side stays a graph edge.

EXPECTED EFFECT
---------------
  * E09 reaches three reasons: STATIC_FIELD_STORE, MONITOR, RETURNED.
  * RETURNED stops being confined to {RETURNED} and starts co-occurring.
  * RETURNED appears on internal/external objects, so it becomes visible under the
    internal+external population without removing that filter.
  * <returnValue,0> nodes keep their own RETURNED from collectReasons; this is additive.
  * NoEscape-with-reasons must stay at its pre-patch value. That is the check that
    distinguishes this patch from the setAsReturn version.

diff -ruN a/src/main/Main.java b/src/main/Main.java
--- a/src/main/Main.java	2026-09-08 11:06:49.655363300 +0530
+++ b/src/main/Main.java	2026-09-08 11:06:49.686406700 +0530
@@ -159,6 +159,11 @@
 	
 			printResForJVM(sr.solvedSummaries, args[2], args[4]);
 
+			// Attribute RETURNED to the concrete objects now that the verdict is known.
+			// Must run after resolution and before printReasons; see the comment on
+			// ReworkedResolver.stampReturned for why it cannot be done at the return site.
+			ReworkedResolver.stampReturned(resolved);
+
 			printReasons(StaticAnalyser.ptgs, resolved, args[2], args[4]);
 		}
 		else {
diff -ruN a/src/resolver/ReworkedResolver.java b/src/resolver/ReworkedResolver.java
--- a/src/resolver/ReworkedResolver.java	2026-09-08 11:06:49.715159600 +0530
+++ b/src/resolver/ReworkedResolver.java	2026-09-08 11:06:49.745120300 +0530
@@ -820,4 +820,57 @@
                 this.solvedSummaries.get(s.getMethod()).put(s.getObject(), new EscapeStatus(es));
         }
     }
+
+    /*
+     * stampReturned - attribute RETURNED to the CONCRETE object, after resolution.
+     *
+     * WHY IT IS NEEDED AT ALL
+     * Today RETURNED is recorded only by collectReasons, and only on a <returnValue,0>
+     * StandardObject. That node exists only if the return's conditional value survived into
+     * the summary, and EscapeStatus.purge() deletes the CV outright whenever the object was
+     * already escaping:
+     *
+     *     if (containsCV()) { if (doesEscape()) purgeAndEscape(); ... }
+     *
+     * So the reason is destroyed exactly when the multiplicity would have exceeded one.
+     * corpus/E09 - returned AND stored to a static AND used as a monitor - reported two
+     * reasons instead of three, and across six benchmarks RETURNED occurred in exactly one
+     * distinct reason set, {RETURNED}.
+     *
+     * WHY IT IS DONE HERE AND NOT AT THE RETURN STATEMENT
+     * The obvious place is PointsToGraph.setAsReturn, which knows the concrete objects and
+     * runs before purge() can destroy anything. That was tried and is WRONG: "flows to the
+     * return slot" does not imply Escape. Under MarkParamReturnEscaping == false a parameter
+     * that is merely returned does not escape - isEscapingParam() explicitly skips return
+     * successors - so recording there put RETURNED on 560 NoEscape objects on avrora (465
+     * parameter, 95 external), breaking the invariant that a non-empty reason set implies
+     * Escape.
+     *
+     * Here the verdict is known, so the doesEscape() guard makes the attribution exact.
+     *
+     * WHY IT STILL WORKS WHEN THE CV WAS PURGED
+     * JReturnStmtHandler.returnedObjects is populated at the return statement itself, from
+     * ptg.vars, and is not an EscapeState. purge() cannot reach it. So the set of returned
+     * objects survives even for the objects whose CV - and whose dependency edge - was
+     * destroyed.
+     *
+     * WHAT IT DOES NOT CHANGE
+     * No EscapeStatus is constructed, mutated or read beyond doesEscape(); no summary entry
+     * is written. The write goes only to the append-only EscapeReasons side map, which
+     * nothing in the analysis reads. The verdict is unchanged by construction, and the .res
+     * line-multiset diff is the test of that rather than the argument for it.
+     */
+    public static void stampReturned(Map<SootMethod, HashMap<ObjectNode, EscapeStatus>> resolved) {
+        if (resolved == null) return;
+        for (Map.Entry<SootMethod, HashSet<ObjectNode>> e
+                : JReturnStmtHandler.returnedObjects.entrySet()) {
+            HashMap<ObjectNode, EscapeStatus> summary = resolved.get(e.getKey());
+            if (summary == null || e.getValue() == null) continue;
+            for (ObjectNode o : e.getValue()) {
+                EscapeStatus es = summary.get(o);
+                if (es != null && es.doesEscape())
+                    EscapeReasons.record(e.getKey(), o, EscapeReason.RETURNED);
+            }
+        }
+    }
 }
